Torchlight: transparent renderables at the same distance from the camera keep the order they
were queued in. `DistanceSortDescendingLess` broke the tie by comparing pass pointers, which is
deterministic within a run but not between runs: the heap's addresses differ (Windows randomises
them; on the console any change to what was allocated before moves them), so two alpha-blended
particle systems at one point - the town lantern's glow and sparkle - drew in either order, and a
frame of the same scene came out 1.8-2.1% different from run to run with the clock pinned
(2026-10-23). Up to 2,000 entries the list is sorted with `std::stable_sort`, so treating the
two as equal keeps the queue's order, which follows the scene graph; beyond that the radix sort
already orders equal distances by pass hash, which is the same every run. Not __SWITCH__-guarded: every target draws the
same frames from the same scene.

diff --git a/OgreMain/src/OgreRenderQueueSortingGrouping.cpp b/OgreMain/src/OgreRenderQueueSortingGrouping.cpp
